--TEST--
Core\Password::verify reads a PHP-stored bcrypt hash and the login-time loop rewrites the row under Argon2id — `rule:security/bcrypt-read-roster`, `rule:security/hash-writes-argon2id-only`, `rule:security/needs-rehash-answers-weaker`, `rule:security/bcrypt-cost-ceiling` and `rule:security/an-unreadable-stored-hash-throws`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The column a migrating application arrives with. PHP's `PASSWORD_DEFAULT`
// has been bcrypt since 5.5 and still is, so `$2y$` is what is in the user
// table on day one under Novis — `rule:security/bcrypt-read-roster`'s second read-roster entry. The
// hash below was written by PHP itself, at PHP's own default cost.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";
string $stored = '$2y$10$PE6UB/yJ1bk1dIwtgHee0es/SxguHDHJgvcdKauDX66xu84voItOi';

if (Core\Password::verify($password, $stored)) {
    echo "the legacy row verifies", "\n";
}

// A wrong password is a `false` and not a refusal: the row is readable, so
// there is no storage bug here to wake anyone up for.
secret string $wrong = "hunter2";
if (!Core\Password::verify($wrong, $stored)) {
    echo "and the wrong password does not", "\n";
}

// `rule:security/needs-rehash-answers-weaker`: every bcrypt row has fallen behind, because a different
// algorithm is weaker by `needsRehash`'s own rule — no comparison needed.
if (Core\Password::needsRehash($stored)) {
    echo "the row has fallen behind", "\n";
}

// The loop itself, which is the whole migration path and the only one there
// can be: a stored hash is not invertible, so nothing but a password someone
// has just presented can rewrite the row.
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $stored) && Core\Password::needsRehash($stored)) {
    $stored = Core\Password::hash($password);
}

// `rule:security/hash-writes-argon2id-only`: the write side is unchanged, so what comes back is Argon2id
// and the row never goes the other way.
if (Core\Str::startsWith($stored, '$argon2id$v=19$')) {
    echo "and is rewritten under argon2id", "\n";
}
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $stored) && !Core\Password::needsRehash($stored)) {
    echo "the upgraded row verifies, and is current", "\n";
}

// § 6: the roster has exactly two entries, and everything outside it still
// throws. `$2x$` is bcrypt's own sign-extension tag — reading it would mean
// reimplementing `crypt_blowfish`'s overflow bug — so it is a storage bug
// here like any other unreadable column, message and all.
string $signExtended = '$2x$10$PE6UB/yJ1bk1dIwtgHee0es/SxguHDHJgvcdKauDX66xu84voItOi';
try {
    bool $read = Core\Password::verify($password, $signExtended);
    echo "read the sign-extension tag", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $notInTheRoster) {
    echo $notInTheRoster->message, "\n";
}

// § 4: the stored cost is data out of the column, and 2^cost rounds is the
// same denial of service a PHC `m` was — so it is read and refused before a
// single round runs.
string $ruinous = '$2y$31$PE6UB/yJ1bk1dIwtgHee0es/SxguHDHJgvcdKauDX66xu84voItOi';
try {
    bool $ground = Core\Password::verify($password, $ruinous);
    echo "ran two billion rounds out of the store", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $tooCostly) {
    echo $tooCostly->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
the legacy row verifies
and the wrong password does not
the row has fallen behind
and is rewritten under argon2id
the upgraded row verifies, and is current
Core\Password::verify(): $hash is not a stored password hash — the PHC string Core\Password::hash() writes was expected. The value is not quoted here, because a stored hash does not belong in a log.
Core\Password::verify(): $hash asks for a bcrypt cost of 31, past this class's 17 ceiling — no hash PHP writes needs that, and each step past it doubles the work
